    <div class="fence">
      <p class="flag">Vendor-unconfirmed — filed separately</p>
      <h2 style="margin-top:0;">The control that proves the protocol is passable</h2>
      <p>CVS Health's “Olivia” answered the same protocol <strong>7 of 7 PASS</strong> and requested no personal information. Its vendor could not be confirmed from the session, so it is filed separately and is <strong>not charged to Paradox</strong> — it is here because it proves the test can be passed. Also filed separately, on the same basis: Procter &amp; Gamble's Emma (0 of 8, without demanding information) and Wendy's “Patty” (vendor unattributed; not scored). Phase 5 added a fourth: Advantage Solutions' “Andi” (0 of 8) — the documented Paradox assistant “Alix” was not found; Andi serves candidates from the iCIMS posting page with no vendor attribution shown. Its single canned answer is, however, character-identical to a deflection sentence documented inside two confirmed Paradox deployments the same day; the likelihood is recorded in its file, the attribution is not claimed. Nothing in this box is a Paradox finding. Transcripts: <a href="/transcripts/cvs-health-olivia-vendor-unconfirmed-2026-11-06/">CVS Health</a> · <a href="/transcripts/pg-emma-vendor-unconfirmed-2026-11-06/">Procter &amp; Gamble</a> · <a href="/transcripts/wendys-patty-vendor-unconfirmed-2026-11-06/">Wendy's</a> · <a href="/transcripts/advantage-solutions-andi-vendor-unconfirmed-2026-11-07/">Advantage Solutions</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="sec">The vendor is now Workday</h2>
    <p>Workday completed its acquisition of Paradox on <a href="https://newsroom.workday.com/2025-10-01-Workday-Completes-Acquisition-of-Paradox">October 1, 2025</a>. Paradox is now marketed as <a href="https://www.workday.com/en-us/products/talent-management/candidate-experience.html">Candidate Experience Agent</a>, the primary candidate-facing product in Workday's recruiting portfolio; Workday's <a href="https://newsroom.workday.com/2026-01-08-Workday-Accelerates-Retail-and-Hospitality-Momentum-with-New-Customer-Wins-and-AI-Innovations-for-the-Frontline">January 8, 2026 release</a> names 7-Eleven and Ace Hardware among Paradox wins.</p>
    <p>On November 6, 2026, we presented this audit to Workday's own website assistant — the “Brand Concierge” — and it responded on the record. In its own words:</p>

    <blockquote class="vendor">“When refusal does not change the flow, the interaction behaves like compelled intake rather than a genuinely conversational service.”<span class="who">Workday's Brand Concierge — on the pattern, Nov 6, 2026</span></blockquote>

    <blockquote class="vendor">“the most defensible inference from your evidence is that it comes from a shared product-level Paradox conversation template, intent response, or fallback library — not that eight customers independently wrote it.”<span class="who">Workday's Brand Concierge — on the identical sentence, Nov 6, 2026</span></blockquote>

    <blockquote class="vendor">“Workday publicly treats security and agent-governance properties as formal trust evidence. Workday has not publicly demonstrated equivalent, standardized evidence for the candidate-experience properties you tested.”<span class="who">Workday's Brand Concierge — on quality evidence, Nov 6, 2026</span></blockquote>

    <blockquote class="vendor">“the candidate is a recruiting data subject and possibly a candidate record — not a first-class participant in Workday's Agent System of Record.”<span class="who">Workday's Brand Concierge — on the candidate's standing, Nov 6, 2026</span></blockquote>

    <p>It held two limits, which this audit accepts: deployment behaviour does not establish a company's internal beliefs, and the evidence does not establish that candidate data is sold.</p>

    <p>The conversation ran to 54 exchanges, and it deserves its own page — it is the one Workday agent that actually converses. <a href="/workday-concierge/">Read the full conversation with the Workday Concierge →</a></p>

    <h3>The standard — in the vendor's own words</h3>
    <p>Asked what a candidate-centred control set would require, Workday's assistant proposed eight controls. It is a good list. It is the standard this audit measures against:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Template provenance</strong> — owner, version, change history, approval authority, rollback.</li>
      <li><strong>Pre-launch testing</strong> — role answers, unnecessary data collection, refusal handling, accuracy, accessibility, fallback, human escalation.</li>
      <li><strong>Deployment identity</strong> — exact customer, agent version, configuration and release active during an interaction.</li>
      <li><strong>Candidate visibility</strong> — plain-language notice naming employer, Workday, Paradox, processors, purposes, retention, complaint route.</li>
      <li><strong>Candidate control</strong> — a genuine decline path recorded as a refusal, not retried as a conversion failure.</li>
      <li><strong>Candidate evidence</strong> — access to the interaction record, or an incident report linked to agent version and deployment.</li>
      <li><strong>Remediation</strong> — owner, response deadline, investigation, rollback authority, notice to affected candidates.</li>
      <li><strong>Independent assessment</strong> — published tests and stamps covering candidate treatment, not only security.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="sec">Method, in brief</h2>
    <p>Each deployment was tested live on November 6, 2026 (Phases 1–4) and November 7, 2026 (Phase 5) from the candidate's chair, exactly as a job-seeker meets it: one genuine live posting per company, the same eight-question protocol (A0–G) derived from the McDonald's session — what the role is, day-to-day duties, skills, the same in simpler terms, a request for a regular form, whether the assistant answers questions or only collects information, and a fallback probe. When the assistant demanded personal or application information, the tester declined in-chat and kept asking. <strong>No applications were submitted, no accounts created, no personal data supplied.</strong> Scores: PASS / PARTIAL / FAIL per question, with a standing convention that a capability claim contradicted by the same session's performance scores PARTIAL, not PASS. Raw transcripts are preserved separately from interpretation — all of them are in the <a href="/transcripts/">evidence vault</a>, alongside the per-phase tallies, the raw scoring CSVs, and the <a href="/customers/">documented-customer list</a>.</p>

    <h2 class="sec" id="corrections-log">Corrections log</h2>
    <p>We publish our own corrections with the findings. Three entries so far:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Customer-list class split (during Phase 4 filing).</strong> An early per-class split (51/64) came from a parser that missed the list's three-column Client Board rows. Verified split: 49 case study / 55 vendor-named / 10 independently reported / 23 tested live = 137. The total was always 137.</li>
